.\" mark(1) — hand-written troff, per M10.
.\"
.\" Not generated from clap. A generator would produce a flag list, and the
.\" half of this page worth reading is the part clap does not know: which
.\" subcommands answer locally and which drive the running app, what each exit
.\" code means to a caller, and where the socket and the session file live.
.\" `cli/src/main.rs`'s `the_man_page_and_completions_name_every_subcommand`
.\" fails if a subcommand or a long flag is added and not documented here, so
.\" the drift a generator prevents is prevented by a test instead.
.\"
.\" Preview it with:  man packaging/mark.1
.\" Lint it with:     mandoc -T lint packaging/mark.1
.TH MARK 1 "2026-09-01" "mark 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mark \- fast native markdown viewer for macOS, and its CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mark
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.RI [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mark
reads, renders, and edits markdown. It is one binary with two halves that share
one implementation of parsing, task semantics, highlighting, and directory
queries: a Rust core, and an AppKit app that links the same core.
.PP
Commands split into two kinds, and the distinction is the one that matters when
scripting:
.TP
.B local
.BR render ,\  diff ,\  toc ,\  tasks ,\  check ,\  normalize ,\  ls ,\  grep ,\  links ,
.BR watch ,\  stats ,\  doctor ,
and
.B theme
with
.BR --list ,\  --show ,
or
.BR --import .
These answer in this process, in milliseconds, with no app running. An agent is
usually in exactly this situation.
.TP
.B remote
.BR open ,\  tab ,\  goto ,\  reload ,\  sidebar ,\  nav ,
and
.B theme
with a name. These drive the running app over a Unix socket at
.IR $TMPDIR/mark-$UID.sock ,
launching it first if it is not running. Every one of them can fail with a
reason and a non-zero exit; none of them steals focus.
.PP
.B --json
is available on every command that carries structured output, and always writes
only JSON to stdout \(em diagnostics go to stderr, so
.B MARK_TRACE=1
cannot corrupt a parse.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Local
.TP
.BI "render " file " [--html|--ansi|--plain] [--prefix " N "] [--theme " name ]
Render to stdout.
.B --html
is a complete self-contained document carrying both light and dark palettes,
inline MathML, and inline SVG for Mermaid diagrams \(em no JavaScript and no
network access. Without a format flag, output is styled when stdout is a
terminal and plain when it is a pipe.
.B --prefix
emits only the first
.I N
top-level blocks, which is the first-paint tunable the app itself uses.
.TP
.BI "diff " [path] " [--html|--ansi|--plain] [--json] [--stat] [--tracked] [--theme " name ]
What changed against git
.BR HEAD .
A
.B file
shows its own changed lines; a
.B directory
\(em or nothing at all \(em shows one row per changed file in the repository,
which is the same pair of meanings
.B open
and
.B ls
already give a path.
.IP
.B --html
is the
.I rendered
diff: a complete self-contained document in which removed blocks are shown in
place, struck through and tinted, and added blocks tinted \(em markdown as
markdown rather than as patch text. It needs a single file, because a directory
has no one document to render.
.B --stat
gives the counts and no hunks.
.B --tracked
leaves untracked files out; by default they are included with every line counted
as added, because a note you just wrote is the most interesting row in a notes
tree.
.IP
Untracked files and binaries are reported honestly rather than numerically: a
binary file that changed reads
.B binary
rather than
.BR "+0 \(mi0" ,
which would be a lie about a file that did change.
.IP
A path that is not in a git repository exits
.B 0
and says so on stderr. That is not a failure \(em a script asking "what changed?"
of a plain directory deserves an empty answer rather than a non-zero exit. A
machine with no
.B git
at all, or with no Command Line Tools, behaves identically.
.TP
.BI "toc " file " [--json] [--insert] [--min-level " N "] [--max-level " N ]
The heading tree: level, text, anchor, byte offsets, line, and block id.
.IP
.B --insert
writes the contents list
.I into
the document, between
.B <!-- toc -->
and
.BR "<!-- /toc -->" .
Both markers must already be there:
.B mark
will not guess where a contents list belongs, and adding two comment lines is a
smaller ask than undoing a wrong insertion. Nothing outside the markers is
touched, so running it again replaces what it wrote and changes nothing else \(em
and a run that would change nothing writes nothing at all, rather than moving the
file's mtime and waking every watcher looking at it.
.IP
The markers are HTML comments, which mark's renderer drops, so they never appear
on the page. Links use the anchors the renderer generated, deduplicated the same
way, so a document with two identically-named headings links to the right one.
The write goes through the same locked, atomic path
.B mark check
uses.
.TP
.BI "tasks " [path] " [--open] [--state " name "] [--tag " tag "] [--priority " N "] \
[--due-before " when "] [--due-after " when "] [--overdue] [--no-due] [--sort " key "] \
[--today " YYYY-MM-DD "] [--json] [--depth " N ]
Every task in a file or directory: path, index in document order, state, text,
and byte span.
.IP
There are five states \(em
.BR [\ ] " open, " [/] " in progress, " [x] " done, " [-] " cancelled, " [?] " blocked"
\(em and one byte between the brackets is all that separates them.
.B --open
lists everything still
.IR outstanding :
open, in progress, or blocked. Cancelled and done are both finished with.
.B --state
is the precise form, takes one of
.BR open ,\  in-progress ,\  done ,\  cancelled ,\  blocked ,
and may be repeated, in which case any of them matches. An unknown name is a
usage error rather than a filter that matches nothing.
.IP
The rest filter on the metadata a task carries in its own text \(em
.BR @tag ,
.BR @due( YYYY-MM-DD ),
.BR @start( ... ),
.BR @done( ... ),
and
.BR ! / !! / !!!
for priority 1 to 3.
.B --tag
takes a tag with or without its
.RB \(oq @ \(cq
and may be repeated;
.B --priority
matches that level
.IR "or higher" ;
.B --no-due
matches tasks with no date at all.
.B --due-before
and
.B --due-after
each take a date, the word
.BR today ,
or a
.BI + N d
offset, and compare strictly.
.B --overdue
is due before today.
.B --sort
orders the answer by
.BR due ,\  priority ,\  state ,
or
.B index
(document order, the default); every key is a stable sort over document order,
so ties come out in the order they would have printed anyway.
.IP
.B --today
is what \(oqtoday\(cq means, for
.B --overdue
and for
.BI + N d
offsets. It defaults to the local date, and it is the
.I only
place in
.B mark
where a date comparison reads a clock \(em rendering never does, so
.B "render --html"
output is reproducible. Naming it explicitly is what makes a filter testable
without freezing the machine's clock.
.TP
.BI "check " file " --item " N " [--on|--off|--toggle|--state " name "] [--stamp] \
[--today " YYYY-MM-DD "] [--json]"
Flip one checkbox in place. Exactly one byte changes \(em the character between
the brackets \(em and the write is temp-file-plus-rename through the real file
behind any symlink. The document is re-parsed immediately before the write, and
if the target span no longer holds a task marker the write is refused rather
than guessed.
.B --toggle
is the default: open becomes done, done becomes open, and any other state
becomes done, because ticking a box ticks it. That makes a toggle a one-way exit
from in progress, cancelled and blocked \(em reaching those is what
.B --state
is for, and it takes any of the five names.
.IP
.B --stamp
additionally appends
.BI @done( YYYY-MM-DD )
to the item when it
.I becomes
done, at an offset taken from the parser rather than from a text search. It is
opt-in because it is the one write in the product that is not a single byte, and
it happens in the
.I same
atomic write as the toggle \(em two writes would take the lock twice and leave a
window where the file says done and does not say when. An item that already
carries a date is not stamped twice.
.B --today
sets the date it writes.
.IP
The write takes an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
first and refuses, with exit status 6, if another
.B mark
holds it \(em typically a window with unsaved edits to the same file. The lock
is never waited on.
.TP
.BI "normalize " file " [--gfm] [--in-place] [--check]"
Rewrite mark's three non-GFM markers as plain GitHub-flavoured markdown, so a
document publishes cleanly, and do it
.IR losslessly :
.B [-]
becomes
.BR "[x] ~~struck~~" ,
which is how a dropped item should read on GitHub, and
.B [/]
and
.B [?]
become
.B [\ ]
carrying a
.B @doing
or
.B @blocked
tag, so the state is still in the file and the degrade is reversible.
.IP
It writes to
.B stdout
by default.
.B --in-place
is required to touch the file, because a verb whose name does not obviously
write should not rewrite a document as its default behaviour; that path goes
through the same atomic write and the same
.BR flock (2)
as every other write, and so exits 6 rather than clobbering a document another
.B mark
holds.
.B --check
reports how many markers would be rewritten and writes nothing anywhere.
.B --gfm
names the only direction there is and is the default; it exists so that adding
the reverse later cannot change what a bare
.B normalize
does. Running it twice is a no-op: an already-struck item is not struck again,
and a tag it would have added is not added twice.
.TP
.BI "ls " [dir] " [--json] [--depth " N "] [--all] [--git]"
Markdown files with titles and outstanding/active task counts \(em where
.I active
excludes cancelled items, so a list you abandoned two of seven items in reads
.BR 3/5 .
With
.B --json
every state is counted separately. Honours
.IR .gitignore .
.B --all
includes non-markdown files.
.B --git
adds each file's changed lines against git
.BR HEAD ,
as
.BR "+12 \(mi3" ,
in the same order the app's sidebar draws them \(em changes first, then the task
count. One
.B git
query for the listing's repository, not one per file. A directory that is not in
a repository simply gains no column.
.TP
.BI "grep " pattern " " [path] " [--json] [-i|--ignore-case] [--depth " N ]
Regular-expression search reporting the heading each match sits under.
.TP
.BI "links " [path] " [--to " FILE ] " [--broken] [--images] [--json] [--depth " N ]
Every link and image, and whether its target is there. A
.I file
reports its own references; a
.I directory
is walked, which is the shape the interesting question takes \(em which of these
notes point at something that is no longer there.
.IP
Destinations are resolved against the
.I document
that holds them, not against the walk root, and are percent\(hydecoded first, so
.B my%20note.md
is looked for under the name it actually has on disk.
.IP
.B --broken
means a
.I local
path that is missing.
.B mark
never fetches an external URL \(em a checker that makes HTTP requests is a
different tool with different failure modes \(em so
.BR http ,\  https ,
and
.B mailto
destinations are reported as
.BR "not checked" ,
and a fragment resolves inside the document. Neither is ever called broken.
.IP
.B --images
narrows to
.BR ![alt](\(...) ,
which is the half the app asks for: the shell serves a document's pictures over
a scheme of its own, and this is the allowlist it serves from.
.IP
.B --to
turns the question around: what points
.I at
this file. Matching is on the resolved path, so a link written from a sibling
directory counts and a document never links to itself. Each row is the linking
document, the heading the link sits under, and the link's own text \(em what the
other document calls this one, which is the useful half when the filename is
already in the argument.
.IP
Exits 0 whether or not anything is broken. The finding is on stdout; a non\(hyzero
exit is reserved for not being able to answer.
.TP
.BI "watch " [path] " [--follow] [--interval " SECONDS "] [--json] [--depth " N ]
Block until a file or directory changes, then say what changed and exit \(em for
the shell loop an agent actually writes:
.IP
.EX
while mark watch notes/; do just build; done
.EE
.IP
One line per change:
.BR created ,
.BR modified ,
or
.B removed
and the path.
.B --follow
keeps going instead, printing each change until interrupted, and flushes every
round so it can be piped into something.
.IP
It polls rather than using FSEvents, for the reason
.I 2026-08-27-sidebar-polls-listed-directories
gives for the sidebar: one
.BR stat (2)
per watched file is cheap, bounded, and has no callback to leak.
.B --interval
has a 50 ms floor, since a zero interval is a busy loop on somebody's battery
rather than a faster answer.
.TP
.BI "stats " file " [--json]"
Per-stage timings and counters for one document: parse, highlight, render,
block count, code bytes, task counts, cache hit rate \(em and how long the
document is in the units a *writer* cares about: words, characters, lines, and
an estimated reading time.
.IP
The word count is taken from the parsed document rather than from its bytes, so
frontmatter, fenced and inline code, math, diagrams, an image's alt text and a
link's URL are none of them counted as prose. That is also why the number the
editor's status bar shows and the one printed here cannot differ.
.TP
.BR doctor " [--json]"
Environment report to paste into a bug report: the resolved socket path and its
length against the 104-byte limit, whether the app is running, the
.I .app
resolved from
.IR $0 ,
core version, theme directory, syntect asset load time, and the
.B git
this build would run.
.IP
That last line is the first thing to check when change badges are missing: a git
failure is deliberately invisible in the interface, so this is where it is
visible instead. "not found" with no Command Line Tools means
.BR "xcode-select --install" .
.IP
It also reports which build this is \(em the commit and commit date under
.BR "built from" ,
stamped in when the binary was compiled \(em and, when an app answers, the
build and bundle path of
.I that
app. Since
.B mark
is installed from a Homebrew
.B --HEAD
tap, the version alone does not identify a build: every install between two
version bumps reports the same one. Two different builds on those two lines is a
CLI and an app from separate installs, which behaves like one product that is
subtly wrong.
.IP
The
.B registered
line counts the bundles LaunchServices holds for
.BR dev.mark.app .
That identifier, the
.B mark://
scheme and the markdown document types have exactly one winner, so more than one
claimant means
.B "open notes.md"
is a coin toss; the winner is marked, and a winner that is not the bundle this
CLI came from is called out. A build in a
.BR "git worktree" (1)
does not register, so it cannot become that winner by accident \(em see
.B MARK_FORCE_LSREGISTER
below.
.IP
With an app running it also prints the window and tab counts, the resident web
views, and the memory formula evaluated over them. The count is web
.IR views ,
not tabs: the markdown reference's window holds one and owns no tab, and the
line names it when it is open.
.SS Remote
.TP
.BI "open " path " [--tab] [--json]"
A file opens in a tab. A
.B directory
roots the sidebar there, exactly as
.B nav
does, and the reply says which of the two happened.
.B --tab
adds the tab without moving the reader off the current document. Neither form
activates the app.
.TP
.BR "tab list" " [--json]"
Every open tab, in every window: index, title, open/total tasks, and whether it
currently holds a web view. With more than one window open, or a window split
into two editor groups, each row is prefixed by its window and group \(em
.BR w0L ", " w0R
\(em where
.B L
and
.B R
are the two halves of a split. Every tab has a group, whether or not it is the
one on screen there. The index is per window, across its groups in order, which is what
.B tab select
takes.
.TP
.BI "tab select " index|path " [--json]"
Bring a tab to the front. An
.I index
addresses the window the command is acting on; a
.I path
is looked for in every window, so a document is found wherever it is.
.TP
.BI "tab close " [index|path] " [--all] [--json]"
Close a tab; with no argument, the selected one.
.B --all
closes every tab in the window being driven, both halves of a split included,
and cannot be combined with naming one. The window stays open on its empty state
\(em it still has its sidebar, and closing every document is not the same
request as closing the window. With
.B --json
the
.B closed
key is an
.I array
here, where closing a single tab makes it one object.
.TP
.BI "goto " anchor " [--json]"
Scroll the front document to a heading, with or without the leading
.RB \(oq # \(cq.
Exits 5 when the document has no such anchor.
.TP
.BR reload " [--json]"
Re-read the front document from disk and patch the page. Refused, rather than
silently discarding work, when the tab has unsaved changes.
.TP
.BR sidebar " [--json]"
Report the sidebar without moving it: root, breadcrumb, how deep the back and
forward history is, what it is showing, and the active filter.
.TP
.BI "nav " dir " | --up (--parent) | --back | --forward [--json]"
Move the sidebar's root: into a directory, up to its parent, or back and forward
through the roots already visited \(em the same four moves the window offers as
Cmd-Up, Cmd-[ and Cmd-]. A move with nowhere to go exits 5 rather than
succeeding quietly.
.TP
.BI "theme " [name] " [--system] [--list] [--show " name "] [--import " file "] [--json]"
Apply a theme to every open tab, or, with no name and no flags, report the one
in force. Both go over the socket.
.IP
A theme is a light/dark pair, and naming one shows
.I that
half:
.B theme solarized-light
is light on a Mac that is in dark mode.
.B --system
goes back to following the system appearance between the two halves \(em with a
name, or on its own to keep the theme you already have.
.IP
.BR --list ,
.BR --show ,
and
.B --import
are answered locally.
.B --import
converts a
.I .tmTheme
or a base16 scheme
.I .yaml
into
.IR ~/.config/mark/themes .
.SH EXIT STATUS
A contract to script against, which is why a usage error is 1 rather than
clap's default 2.
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage: an unknown flag, or an argument combination this tool refuses.
.TP
.B 2
A file that is missing or cannot be read.
.TP
.B 3
A task index out of range, or a task marker that moved between the read and the
write. Nothing was written.
.IP
Note that a task's index is its position in document order among the tasks
.B mark
recognises, and that set grew when
.BR [/] ,
.B [-]
and
.B [?]
became markers. A document that gains one of those renumbers every task below
it, so an index held across such an edit \(em in a script, or in an
already-printed listing \(em is stale. Re-read
.B tasks
rather than reusing an index across a change to the file.
.TP
.B 4
The app could not be reached: no socket, a launch that failed, a reply that
never came. Worth retrying.
.TP
.B 5
The app was reached and refused \(em no such anchor, no such tab, nothing to go
back to. Not worth retrying.
.TP
.B 6
Another
.B mark
holds the document's write lock: a window with unsaved edits, or another
.B mark
mid\-write. Nothing was written, and the message names the pid holding it. The
lock is
.BR flock (2)
and so is released the instant the holder exits, however it exits. Worth
retrying once that happens.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MARK_TRACE
Set to 1 for per-stage timings on stderr, and, in the app, per-stage logging to
.BR OSLog .
Never on stdout.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the socket lives. The full socket path must stay under 104 bytes
.RI ( sun_path
on macOS, not Linux's 108); the app refuses to start rather than truncating it.
.TP
.B MARK_APP
An explicit
.I mark.app
to talk to and to launch, instead of the one resolved from
.IR $0 .
.TP
.B MARK_NO_LAUNCH
Set to 1 to fail with exit 4 instead of launching the app.
.TP
.BR MARK_NO_LSREGISTER ", " MARK_FORCE_LSREGISTER
Read by
.IR scripts/assemble-bundle.sh ,
not by this CLI. The first suppresses the LaunchServices registration of a
freshly assembled bundle \(em the Homebrew formula sets it, and registers the
installed copy afterwards, because the one built here is in a temp directory
brew then deletes. The second registers anyway from a linked
.BR "git worktree" (1),
where the default is not to: a registration outlives the directory it names, so
a pruned worktree would go on answering
.B "open notes.md"
from a path that is gone. Undo one with
.BR "lsregister -u " path .
.TP
.BR MARK_IPC_TIMEOUT_MS ", " MARK_IPC_LAUNCH_TIMEOUT_MS
Reply timeout, and how long to wait for a cold launch. Default 30000 and 15000.
.TP
.B MARK_PROTOCOL_VERSION
The wire version to claim. For exercising the version-mismatch path.
.TP
.B MARK_THEME_DIR
Where user themes are read from, instead of
.IR ~/.config/mark/themes .
.TP
.B MARK_SESSION_FILE
Where the app reads and writes its session, instead of
.IR "~/Library/Application Support/mark/session.json" .
.TP
.B MARK_NO_SESSION
Set to 1 and the app neither restores nor saves a session.
.TP
.B MARK_RESIDENT_TABS
How many live web views the app keeps, across every window. Default 3; each one
costs about 52 MB, so the footprint is roughly 100 MB plus 52 MB per resident
view. Opening a window does not raise the budget, and neither does the markdown
reference \(em its window holds one of these views and spends from the same
limit. A tab that is on screen \(em either group of a split, in any window \(em
or has unsaved changes is never dehydrated, so the working set can exceed this;
see
.B doctor
for what it actually is.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Terminal width for
.BR render ,
overriding what the terminal reports.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/mark-$UID.sock
The command socket, mode 0600, uid-checked. Recreated on launch; a stale one is
unlinked and rebound.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/mark/session.json
Tabs, selection, scroll offsets, sidebar root and history, theme, and whether
the editor pane was open. Written atomically.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mark/themes/*.toml
User themes, picked up without a rebuild.
.TP
.I mark.app/Contents/Resources/markdown-reference.md
Every markdown construct the renderer supports, with the source next to the
result. The app shows it under Help, Markdown Reference; it is an ordinary
markdown file, so
.B mark render
prints it in a terminal too.
.TP
.I mark.app/Contents/MacOS/mark-cli
This program.
.B mark
on your PATH is a symlink to it, and it resolves
.I $0
through the symlink chain to find the bundle \(em so the link breaks if the
.I .app
moves.
.SH NOTES
The app is not sandboxed and there is no telemetry of any kind. Documents are
read where they are; nothing is uploaded, and no file contents are ever logged.
.PP
The write lock is
.BR flock (2)
and therefore
.IR advisory .
It stops one
.B mark
clobbering another; it does not stop
.BR vim ,
VS Code, or anything else, none of which take it or respect it. An external
editor saving over a document the app holds unsaved still raises the app's
keep\-mine / take\-theirs conflict prompt, which is the only defence against
those. A document on an NFS or SMB mount, where
.BR flock (2)
is unreliable, is effectively unlocked.
.PP
.B mark://
URLs are registered with LaunchServices as the cold-launch and Finder path, and
carry the same commands:
.IR mark://open?path=/n.md&tab=1 .
.SH SEE ALSO
The decision records under
.IR docs/adrs/ ,
which carry the reasoning and the measurements behind all of the above, and
.I packaging/README.md
for installation.
